WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.490
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:10.630
We recently talked about an amazing paper
that uses mixture theory to simulate the interaction

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.880
of liquids and fabrics.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.900
And this new work is about simulating fluid
flows where we have some debris or other foreign

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.060
particles in our liquids.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.020
This is really challenging.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:28.020
For example, one of the key challenges is
incorporating two-way coupling into this process.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:33.350
This means that the sand is allowed to have
an effect on the fluid, but at the same time,

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:38.090
as the fluid sloshes around, it also moves
the sand particles within.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:42.859
Now, before you start wondering whether this
is real footage or not, the fact that this

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:48.079
is a simulation should become clear now, because
what you see here in the background is where

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.059
the movement of the two domains are shown
in isolation.

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:56.309
Just look at how much interaction there is
between the two.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:57.309
Unbelievable.

00:00:57.309 --> 00:01:00.129
Beautiful simulation, ice cream for your eyes.

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:05.230
This new method also contains a novel density
correction step, and if you watch closely

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.890
here, you’ll notice why.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:08.890
Got it?

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.460
Let’s watch it again.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.720
If we try to run this elastoplastic simulation
for these two previous methods, they introduce

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:22.370
here a gain in density, or in other words,
we end up with more stuff than we started

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.660
with.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.340
These two rows show the number of particles
in the simulation in the worst case scenario,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.980
and as you see, some of these incorporate
millions of particles for the fluid and many

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.610
hundreds of thousands for the sediment.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:42.490
Since this work uses the material point method,
which is a hybrid simulation technique that

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:49.240
uses both particles and grids, the delta x
row denotes the resolution of the simulation

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.330
grid.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:56.790
Now since these grids are often used for 3D
simulations, we need to raise the 256 and

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:03.190
the 512 to the third power, and with that,
we get a simulation grid with up to hundreds

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:08.819
of millions of points, and we haven’t even
talked about the particle representation yet!

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:14.060
In the face of all of these challenges, the
simulator is able to compute one frame in

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.230
a matter of minutes, and not hours or days,
which is an incredible feat.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:23.769
With this, I think it is easy to see that
the computer graphics research is improving

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:25.739
at a staggering pace.

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:27.310
What a time to be alive!

